  "result": "I added `split(cents, parts)` to `shop/money.py:23` and a new test file. All 11 tests pass, including the 5 new ones.\n\n```python\ndef split(cents: int, parts: int) -> list[int]:\n    \"\"\"Split cents into parts; the remainder goes one cent each to the first parts.\"\"\"\n    if parts < 1:\n        raise ValueError(f\"parts must be at least 1: {parts}\")\n    base, extra = divmod(cents, parts)\n    return [base + 1 if i < extra else base for i in range(parts)]\n```\n\n- **Tests:** there was no money test file, so I created `tests/test_money.py` in the same `unittest` style as `tests/test_pricing.py`. It covers an even split, the remainder going to the first elements (`split(10, 3) == [4, 3, 3]`), a single part, zero cents, and `ValueError` when `parts` is 0 or negative. I ran them with `python3 -m unittest discover -s tests -t .`.\n- **Negative amounts:** your spec doesn't cover these. The function still returns parts that add up to `cents`, but `split(-10, 3)` gives `[-3, -3, -4]`, so the extra cent ends up on the last element. If you'd rather the first element take it, or want negatives rejected, that's a one-line change.\n\nThe changes aren't committed.",
